    "page.colourCode": "<b>Colour code, the same everywhere:</b> in the network figures, colour shows what happens to cells, connections and signals: green, on time and in agreement; vermilion, late; grey, failed or cut off; blue, reached by a signal spreading from a stimulus; purple, a connection running back towards the senses; orange, the anaesthetic. Stimulated sensory cells take the colour of the main text, and wiring drawn with nothing simulated takes the softer colour of secondary text. The colours are based on the Okabe–Ito palette, chosen to stay distinguishable with colour blindness (Wong, 2011). Every figure has a key naming each colour and shape it uses, and charts have keys of their own. Test results give the paper's verdict first, in words and with a mark (✓ passed, ✕ failed, ◐ differs by organism, – a check, an observation or no pass rule), then the number of runs in which the statement held; point at a result for a plain explanation.",
